1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,160
You may be seated.

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:29,440
Well, good morning again.

3
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:34,240
Last week, we launched a new series called What Does the Bible Say About?

4
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:37,600
We received lots of questions from you all over 20.

5
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:39,500
We will not have time to answer all of them.

6
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:48,300
I told you last week that I was very encouraged that you guys did not try and back me into a hot-button cultural topic corner for the most part.

7
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:52,240
You asked well-thought-out, nuanced theological questions.

8
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:56,020
Some of you are new believers wrestling with the basic truths of Christianity.

9
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:57,740
Those are all great questions.

10
00:00:57,860 --> 00:01:04,300
I did receive one difficult, challenging, maybe a bit of a hot-button cultural topic.

11
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:13,700
And rather than covering it myself, I decided to assign it to somebody else so that if they say something you don't like, I can just pretend like I disagree with it and we can move on.

12
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:16,280
Yeah, sounds great, right?

13
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:21,760
So, I want to, in just a moment, welcome to the stage someone who has become a good friend of mine.

14
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:27,080
His name is Dr. Reverend, I think you do that backwards, Reverend Dr. Stephen Cole.

15
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:34,620
He is the director, the kind of CEO of our denomination, our family of churches, and sometimes I like to call him the Pope.

16
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:36,800
He does not like that very much.

17
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:43,180
And what's cool about our family of churches is that it's really non-hierarchical.

18
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:43,640
Non?

19
00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:45,140
That's a really hard word to say.

20
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:45,760
You know what I mean?

21
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:46,700
Non-hierarchical.

22
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:55,960
And we function as a family, and so more than a CEO, Stephen really functions like a pastor to all of our pastors.

23
00:01:55,960 --> 00:01:58,820
And so, he has become a friend of mine.

24
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:07,360
I remember the first time I saw his name come across my caller ID out of the blue, and I answered and said, oh, no, what did I do and how bad is it?

25
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:11,980
And he said, even if you were in trouble, I really couldn't do much about it.

26
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:15,160
So, that was very comforting and encouraging to me.

27
00:02:16,560 --> 00:02:22,080
So, I'd love for Canvas, for all of you, to give a very warm welcome to my good friend, Stephen Cole.

28
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:38,300
I have called him multiple times, and he was in trouble every time.

29
00:02:38,300 --> 00:02:40,560
Can you hear me?

30
00:02:40,560 --> 00:02:43,040
Did you turn it off?

31
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:45,720
I didn't turn it off, but I told him I was.

32
00:02:45,980 --> 00:02:48,540
And then I just told him that I would, and there it is.

33
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:49,400
So, there you go.

34
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:51,780
Joel and I are friends.

35
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:52,980
We'll get to that in a minute.

36
00:02:52,980 --> 00:03:09,440
So, it is funny to call me the Pope, because I have the least amount of papal authority ever in the history of authority when it comes to ecclesial things, church things.

37
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:11,380
But we are a relational people.

38
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:15,960
I like to tell people, on the hierarchy of the Brethren Church, you're a part of that.

39
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:17,640
If you do not know that, it's a denomination.

40
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:19,400
We'll get into that in just a second.

41
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:25,160
There is a small speed bump in the middle, where sometimes, not all the time, I get to stand.

42
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,780
It's a very small, very flat structure.

43
00:03:29,780 --> 00:03:33,060
So, can I come in here and remove anyone?

44
00:03:33,060 --> 00:03:33,580
No.

45
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:38,440
Can I come in here and do anything about anything you said that's crazy?

46
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:39,500
No.

47
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:48,820
Except for, I get to come be your friend and your brother in Christ, and maybe influence you to a different way of thinking, if that was something that was needed.

48
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:52,340
So, that is the authority that I carry.

49
00:03:52,340 --> 00:03:55,800
I tend to tell people, my authority rises and falls with my staff.

50
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:57,500
They're the only ones I can fire.

51
00:03:57,500 --> 00:03:58,120
That's it.

52
00:03:58,120 --> 00:04:00,480
Everybody else is fine.

53
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:03,360
So, even if I called you, you'll be fine.

54
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:04,500
You'll be fine.

55
00:04:04,500 --> 00:04:06,560
So, my name is Stephen Cole.

56
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:08,100
I am the executive director.

57
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:09,200
That's the official title.

58
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:09,820
It isn't Pope.

59
00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:12,440
And I've been here a few times.

60
00:04:12,440 --> 00:04:14,860
If you've been here when I have preached before, raise your hand.

61
00:04:14,860 --> 00:04:16,840
I didn't think that was.

62
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:17,760
I thought that was going to happen.

63
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:18,600
About five of you.

64
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:18,900
Great.

65
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:36,740
So, my role is to be a support, to come alongside, to be a brother in Christ, a minister in the gospel with you, alongside you, with a staff of people that do the same and come together to do the work of the kingdom.

66
00:04:36,740 --> 00:04:37,820
That is my job.

67
00:04:37,820 --> 00:04:40,780
And so, I get to do that around the United States.

68
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:42,860
I get to travel in a lot of places.

69
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:48,980
And we have mission points in 11 countries, if you count America, 11 countries around the world.

70
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:56,520
And so, I get to be a part of that as well and travel and be a part of what it means to do ministry together as a body of Christ.

71
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:57,880
The body of Christ.

72
00:04:57,880 --> 00:05:01,880
Not a local, not just a regional, but the global body of Christ.

73
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:15,940
So, I get to do that on your behalf and bring greetings to you on behalf of other churches and take your story with me when I go tell your story to other people who need encouragement about what the Lord is doing.

74
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:17,740
So, that is my job.

75
00:05:17,740 --> 00:05:24,000
And also, to preach really hard, stupid stuff when a guy calls on the phone and says, here, come preach this.

76
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,120
So, let me ground you in a couple things.

77
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:29,780
Let me tell you a little bit about myself.

78
00:05:30,340 --> 00:05:39,260
You're not going to hear a word I say if you don't trust me, know me, which will be really tough to do since I'm not even sitting next to you as I'm telling you these things.

79
00:05:39,260 --> 00:05:40,720
But I will give it a try.

80
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:43,840
Because I'm going to say some things you're going to be like, yes.

81
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:45,220
And some things are like, no.

82
00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:47,260
Like, we're going to be all over the map today.

83
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:49,140
Maybe we'll resonate 100%.

84
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:50,620
Maybe it'll be 2%.

85
00:05:50,620 --> 00:05:51,280
I don't know.

86
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:52,840
But we'll find out.

87
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:56,160
But the first grounding is this.

88
00:05:56,160 --> 00:06:00,140
If you follow after Jesus Christ, you and I are brothers and sisters.

89
00:06:00,140 --> 00:06:01,220
Period.

90
00:06:01,220 --> 00:06:03,040
Period.

91
00:06:03,040 --> 00:06:03,800
End of sentence.

92
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:05,540
Hard stop.

93
00:06:06,580 --> 00:06:16,660
Anything I say from here on out is about what it is for me to live out my faith and come alongside other men and women to figure out how to do that faithfully together.

94
00:06:16,660 --> 00:06:19,760
So, sometimes we agree.

95
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:20,620
Sometimes we don't agree.

96
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:23,920
If we all agreed all the time, there would not be a Brethren Church denomination.

97
00:06:23,920 --> 00:06:25,340
There would just be a church.

98
00:06:25,340 --> 00:06:28,920
But through history, we have not agreed.

99
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:29,480
Right?

100
00:06:29,480 --> 00:06:32,520
So, a little bit about me.

101
00:06:32,520 --> 00:06:34,560
I was born in Texas.

102
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:36,340
Yep.

103
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:37,140
Yep.

104
00:06:37,140 --> 00:06:40,360
I wear cowboy boots on purpose.

105
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:41,700
I'm wearing them now.

106
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:43,700
Not because I wanted to preach it.

107
00:06:43,700 --> 00:06:46,140
Because I wear cowboy boots on purpose all the time.

108
00:06:46,140 --> 00:06:48,600
It grounds me.

109
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:50,260
It reminds me where I'm from.

110
00:06:50,260 --> 00:06:52,260
I haven't lived in Texas since 1998.

111
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:54,000
It's been a minute.

112
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:59,880
I've actually lived in the city I live in now longer than I've ever lived anywhere else in my whole life.

113
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:02,500
And so, groundedness matters.

114
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:03,020
I'm going to cry.

115
00:07:03,020 --> 00:07:03,260
Sorry.

116
00:07:03,260 --> 00:07:05,100
Groundedness matters.

117
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:05,800
Where you're from.

118
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:06,460
What you know.

119
00:07:06,460 --> 00:07:07,100
Who you are.

120
00:07:07,100 --> 00:07:10,520
The people that speak into your life matters.

121
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:12,540
You are all from here.

122
00:07:12,540 --> 00:07:13,320
Or maybe not.

123
00:07:13,320 --> 00:07:15,120
I mean, it is really close to D.C.

124
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:17,580
I'm going to guess a lot of you guys are transplants.

125
00:07:17,580 --> 00:07:18,180
Would that be fair?

126
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:19,520
Yeah?

127
00:07:21,320 --> 00:07:23,380
So, that's where I was born.

128
00:07:23,380 --> 00:07:24,100
I was born in Texas.

129
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:26,160
My dad was an oil field worker.

130
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:27,760
So, he moved us around a lot.

131
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:29,320
It felt like I was in the military.

132
00:07:29,320 --> 00:07:30,280
We bounced a lot.

133
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:31,480
New Mexico.

134
00:07:31,480 --> 00:07:33,560
Wherever the oil field took him, we went.

135
00:07:35,160 --> 00:07:38,260
So, I've literally lived in the longest place in my whole life right now.

136
00:07:38,260 --> 00:07:39,260
And that's nine years.

137
00:07:39,260 --> 00:07:41,500
Nine years.

138
00:07:41,500 --> 00:07:45,320
Where are your parents?

139
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:46,300
Who are your parents?

140
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:47,460
Who raised you?

141
00:07:47,460 --> 00:07:51,920
Who are the people that spoke to you as you were being formed as a person?

142
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:53,740
Let's say what the Bible calls that.

143
00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:54,840
Discipleship.

144
00:07:55,640 --> 00:07:57,500
Who shaped you as a person?

145
00:07:57,500 --> 00:08:02,660
Not just your parents, but also the community that you grew up in.

146
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:05,500
So, I grew up in a western part of the state of Texas.

147
00:08:05,500 --> 00:08:07,500
The northwest in the panhandle.

148
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:09,340
If you've ever seen Texas, it looks like this.

149
00:08:09,340 --> 00:08:10,740
I grew up here on the knuckle.

150
00:08:10,740 --> 00:08:11,580
Okay?

151
00:08:11,580 --> 00:08:13,200
Lubbock.

152
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:14,160
I was born in Lubbock.

153
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:16,180
I didn't go to Texas Tech.

154
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:17,180
I went to Texas A&M.

155
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:18,600
Joel loves that, by the way.

156
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:20,480
By the way, Doc, I think they call you Doc.

157
00:08:20,480 --> 00:08:21,520
I heard him call you Doc.

158
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:24,100
Doc's a stupid Texas University fan.

159
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:24,920
I don't know why.

160
00:08:25,380 --> 00:08:26,780
He has thought to think.

161
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:36,060
The best thing about it is I can shout really loud if I wanted to.

162
00:08:36,060 --> 00:08:38,280
But here's the best part.

163
00:08:38,280 --> 00:08:40,040
Doc, I think we like each other.

164
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:41,940
We've known each other for about half a minute now.

165
00:08:41,940 --> 00:08:46,240
It's okay that he went to that school.

166
00:08:46,240 --> 00:08:47,780
We'll move on.

167
00:08:47,780 --> 00:08:52,240
But that shapes who you are, where you're from.

168
00:08:54,020 --> 00:08:55,620
My dad was in the oil field.

169
00:08:55,620 --> 00:08:56,800
We saw a lot of that.

170
00:08:56,800 --> 00:08:59,000
New Mexico, back into Texas.

171
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:03,980
So it doesn't feel very southern where I grew up.

172
00:09:03,980 --> 00:09:05,780
It feels very western where I grew up.

173
00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:07,320
It's kind of hard.

174
00:09:07,320 --> 00:09:11,060
You take Texas, divide it down the middle of the state through Dallas.

175
00:09:11,060 --> 00:09:14,280
And the more east and south you go, the more southern it feels.

176
00:09:14,340 --> 00:09:17,020
You get closer to Louisiana, the more south and western you feel.

177
00:09:17,020 --> 00:09:19,780
It feels more like Mexico because you get closer to Mexico.

178
00:09:19,780 --> 00:09:23,740
And the further you get up in north and west to the corner, the more western it feels.

179
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:27,420
And Arizona and Colorado and New Mexico kind of feels like that.

180
00:09:27,420 --> 00:09:28,620
So it's a big place.

181
00:09:29,620 --> 00:09:34,740
I did not really experience racism in my life until I moved to the state of Georgia.

182
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:38,380
Because everybody in my town was either brown or white.

183
00:09:38,380 --> 00:09:42,040
They were from Mexico because the line moved on them in 1845.

184
00:09:43,160 --> 00:09:44,840
Or they were white like me.

185
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:48,940
There were two African American students in my high school.

186
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:51,040
In four years that I was there.

187
00:09:51,040 --> 00:09:51,960
Two.

188
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:54,200
Everybody was brown and white.

189
00:09:54,200 --> 00:09:55,540
Wasn't a big deal.

190
00:09:55,540 --> 00:09:58,580
Then I moved to Georgia and life changed for me.

191
00:09:58,580 --> 00:09:59,400
Eyes were open.

192
00:09:59,400 --> 00:09:59,960
It was shaped.

193
00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:03,220
There was something different about black and white than brown and white.

194
00:10:03,220 --> 00:10:06,460
Not that it's not racist everywhere in the world because there are.

195
00:10:06,460 --> 00:10:08,260
But I wasn't shaped by that.

196
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:15,380
So our traditions, what do we practice?

197
00:10:15,380 --> 00:10:20,880
The things that shape us, they shape who we are and what we do and how we talk and what we think about.

198
00:10:20,880 --> 00:10:24,440
One of my favorite things about this job is I get to go around a lot of different places.

199
00:10:24,440 --> 00:10:25,480
We all talk different.

200
00:10:25,480 --> 00:10:29,080
I grew up saying y'all, right?

201
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:33,320
I talk to people about Virginia depending on what Virginian I talk to.

202
00:10:33,320 --> 00:10:34,300
Sometimes they're southern.

203
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:38,460
Sometimes they're some weird like Atlantic thing, coast thing.

204
00:10:38,460 --> 00:10:39,340
Right?

205
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:42,740
Because it's that weird mix of being in the south and not.

206
00:10:43,580 --> 00:10:48,140
Go to Pennsylvania, they say yunes or some form of that weird word.

207
00:10:48,140 --> 00:10:49,340
Right?

208
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:52,880
A lot of my examples are around food.

209
00:10:52,880 --> 00:10:54,880
I went to one lady's house in Pennsylvania.

210
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:56,960
She served up something she called chili.

211
00:10:56,960 --> 00:10:59,360
There were way too many ingredients for it to be chili.

212
00:10:59,360 --> 00:11:00,740
It was more like soup.

213
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:01,900
But she called it chili.

214
00:11:01,900 --> 00:11:02,480
That's fine.

215
00:11:02,480 --> 00:11:03,260
It's not.

216
00:11:03,260 --> 00:11:05,320
If it has anything but meat in it, it's not chili.

217
00:11:05,320 --> 00:11:06,480
That's the argument I'll make.

218
00:11:08,540 --> 00:11:10,960
She gives out some cornbread, which I was excited about.

219
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:12,760
And then she put maple syrup on the table.

220
00:11:12,760 --> 00:11:15,660
Like, that's an abomination, people.

221
00:11:15,660 --> 00:11:18,900
But she did it.

222
00:11:18,900 --> 00:11:19,900
And she poured it on top.

223
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:22,160
And there was one guy with me who grew up in Northeast Ohio.

224
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:23,400
And he was super pumped.

225
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:24,900
And he slathered syrup.

226
00:11:24,900 --> 00:11:26,120
And he was excited.

227
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:27,360
And I'm like, you're fired.

228
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:29,840
You're fired from this staff.

229
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:32,280
Who eats like that?

230
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:36,020
These things shape us.

231
00:11:36,020 --> 00:11:37,160
They're cultural.

232
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:40,120
They're traditional.

233
00:11:40,120 --> 00:11:41,900
They're rootedness.

234
00:11:41,900 --> 00:11:43,800
There's stuff about these things.

235
00:11:43,800 --> 00:11:50,740
And let me tell you, culture and rootedness, each strategy for breakfast every single day.

236
00:11:50,740 --> 00:11:56,240
There's nothing you can come to with a strategy to think you're going to change a people.

237
00:11:56,240 --> 00:12:01,380
If you don't address culture, if you don't address rootedness, you'll never change it.

238
00:12:01,380 --> 00:12:02,180
Period.

239
00:12:02,180 --> 00:12:04,020
Period.

240
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:05,240
Period.

241
00:12:05,240 --> 00:12:12,720
But my culture, my heritage, where I'm from, where I grew up, is not the most important thing about me.

242
00:12:12,720 --> 00:12:16,140
The most important thing about me is whose I am.

243
00:12:16,140 --> 00:12:17,880
The fact that I follow Jesus Christ.

244
00:12:17,880 --> 00:12:19,140
That's the most important thing.

245
00:12:19,140 --> 00:12:20,980
That's the stuff that should shape me.

246
00:12:20,980 --> 00:12:23,360
That's the culture that should shape who I am.

247
00:12:23,360 --> 00:12:25,860
Over these other things.

248
00:12:25,860 --> 00:12:28,700
But that's really a tough ask.

249
00:12:28,700 --> 00:12:31,360
But the Lord can do it.

250
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:39,340
The Civil War will tell you that before the Civil War, you were a Virginian, a Texan, a Georgian, an Ohioan first.

251
00:12:39,340 --> 00:12:41,500
Then you were an American after that.

252
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:46,280
But when Abraham Lincoln did, I know where I'm at, so bear with me.

253
00:12:46,280 --> 00:12:53,520
When Abraham Lincoln changed things and started talking about union, yeah, come on now.

254
00:12:53,520 --> 00:12:57,620
There's a man belching back there from Alabama.

255
00:12:57,840 --> 00:12:58,500
I can hear him.

256
00:12:58,500 --> 00:13:02,360
Started talking about union.

257
00:13:02,360 --> 00:13:05,000
He began to change the conversation.

258
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:12,980
And so it's after Abraham Lincoln, not before, that we're American first and we're all just where are you from kind of story second.

259
00:13:12,980 --> 00:13:15,500
That's after the Civil War.

260
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:20,580
Before the Civil War, most people didn't go 15 or 20 miles away from their home ever.

261
00:13:21,800 --> 00:13:23,060
Ever, ever, ever.

262
00:13:23,060 --> 00:13:28,940
And then the war started and it took men a long ways away from their homes.

263
00:13:28,940 --> 00:13:32,120
You couldn't leave.

264
00:13:32,120 --> 00:13:35,820
So this conversation, rooted before we go to the next thing, is this.

265
00:13:35,820 --> 00:13:44,900
The best thing, the early church had difficult uphill battle because they were fighting against a culture that knew nothing about them and hated them, right?

266
00:13:47,660 --> 00:13:50,460
But in that world, prior to the Civil War was even true.

267
00:13:50,460 --> 00:13:54,040
So even in our time today, people didn't leave.

268
00:13:54,040 --> 00:13:58,280
So when conversations got hard, you had two choices.

269
00:13:58,280 --> 00:14:00,480
Don't talk to your neighbor ever again.

270
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:01,940
P.S. could be your brother.

271
00:14:01,940 --> 00:14:04,860
Or you worked it out.

272
00:14:04,860 --> 00:14:07,840
We don't live in that culture.

273
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:15,020
I've lived in five states, nine cities, like 22 houses in my lifetime.

274
00:14:16,900 --> 00:14:22,840
If I don't like what I'm about to say, if you don't like what I'm about to say, you can go pack up and go down the street to another church.

275
00:14:22,840 --> 00:14:27,500
The church doesn't have to really fight through this stuff anymore.

276
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:30,760
Because there's just another place to go.

277
00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:40,500
And so we always are looking for some kind of homogeneity about who we are because we think that's going to help my church experience, help my place.

278
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:43,380
Because I feel safe because you think exactly like I do.

279
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:47,560
Prior to the modern era, that didn't exist.

280
00:14:47,560 --> 00:14:49,520
You didn't leave.

281
00:14:49,520 --> 00:14:55,100
So I ask you to listen.

282
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:57,080
Know that I love you.

283
00:14:57,080 --> 00:14:59,480
And know that this matters.

284
00:14:59,480 --> 00:15:01,160
Love each other.

285
00:15:01,160 --> 00:15:05,680
Resist the urge with every fiber in your being to leave.

286
00:15:06,640 --> 00:15:07,160
Resist it.

287
00:15:07,160 --> 00:15:09,880
Because that's not what the kingdom has called us to.

288
00:15:09,880 --> 00:15:13,100
It's not called us to that.

289
00:15:13,100 --> 00:15:16,360
So, you might like some of this stuff.

290
00:15:16,360 --> 00:15:16,860
You might not.

291
00:15:16,860 --> 00:15:19,680
Now, your spiritual heritage.

292
00:15:19,680 --> 00:15:21,400
Most of you have one.

293
00:15:21,400 --> 00:15:23,920
If you've grown up in the church anywhere, shape, or form.

294
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:25,220
You guys live in the south.

295
00:15:25,220 --> 00:15:28,260
It's pretty hard to probably never have run into it.

296
00:15:28,940 --> 00:15:35,480
I used to always like to say when I was not in Georgia anymore, everybody in Georgia has been to church because their grandmama took them to church.

297
00:15:35,480 --> 00:15:36,680
Right?

298
00:15:37,220 --> 00:15:39,900
Now, they may not follow Jesus, but they know who he is.

299
00:15:39,900 --> 00:15:42,040
It's a cultural understanding.

300
00:15:42,040 --> 00:15:42,820
Who is Jesus?

301
00:15:42,820 --> 00:15:44,200
I moved to California.

302
00:15:44,200 --> 00:15:49,060
I meet a guy one time who had never, did not have a clue who Moses was.

303
00:15:49,060 --> 00:15:51,100
Who Abraham was.

304
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:52,200
Who Adam was.

305
00:15:52,200 --> 00:15:53,540
What the flood was.

306
00:15:53,540 --> 00:15:56,620
And I'm like going, I don't have the mental capacity to.

307
00:15:57,360 --> 00:15:59,380
I've never met somebody like that before.

308
00:15:59,380 --> 00:16:02,900
So, there are places in the world where people are clean slates.

309
00:16:02,900 --> 00:16:03,980
It's probably not here.

310
00:16:03,980 --> 00:16:07,100
So, you have a tradition.

311
00:16:07,100 --> 00:16:08,840
You were shaped by that tradition.

312
00:16:08,840 --> 00:16:14,480
Every spiritual tradition has the things that they believe and they love and they care about those things.

313
00:16:14,480 --> 00:16:16,340
The Brethren Church is one of those traditions.

314
00:16:16,340 --> 00:16:21,140
If you did not know what that was before you walked in, there's two streams that feed the Brethren Church.

315
00:16:21,140 --> 00:16:22,400
An Anabaptist stream.

316
00:16:22,400 --> 00:16:24,440
I didn't say anti-Baptist.

317
00:16:24,440 --> 00:16:26,840
Anna, A-N-A, Baptist.

318
00:16:26,980 --> 00:16:28,900
It means re-baptizer in German.

319
00:16:28,900 --> 00:16:31,500
It was a point of making fun of them.

320
00:16:31,500 --> 00:16:39,520
The Germans who were Lutheran at the time thought that baptizing people as believers was actually not what the Scripture told them to do.

321
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:41,980
They actually persecuted the Anabaptists.

322
00:16:41,980 --> 00:16:43,340
The re-baptizers.

323
00:16:43,340 --> 00:16:45,440
They called them that on purpose.

324
00:16:45,440 --> 00:16:47,620
It was kind of a derogatory term for them.

325
00:16:47,620 --> 00:16:49,700
We are part of that stream.

326
00:16:49,700 --> 00:16:54,340
If you grew up in a Baptist church, you baptized believers.

327
00:16:54,340 --> 00:16:55,560
That's part of that.

328
00:16:55,600 --> 00:17:00,260
That all came out of that stream of the radical reformation from the Anabaptist tradition.

329
00:17:00,260 --> 00:17:03,020
You're also part of a pietist tradition.

330
00:17:03,020 --> 00:17:04,380
Most churches are pietistic.

331
00:17:04,380 --> 00:17:07,420
It just simply means that you believe your faith is personal.

332
00:17:07,420 --> 00:17:12,100
It is something that you can wrestle with on your own inside of yourselves.

333
00:17:12,100 --> 00:17:15,520
There's an inside, outward experience with Christ.

334
00:17:15,520 --> 00:17:17,040
Pietistic.

335
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:18,140
There's a bunch of them.

336
00:17:18,140 --> 00:17:19,780
Baptists, Methodists, Lutherans.

337
00:17:19,780 --> 00:17:20,900
We, all of us.

338
00:17:20,900 --> 00:17:24,520
Most traditions are pietistic.

339
00:17:28,300 --> 00:17:36,260
So, we live at this confluence of believing that the Anabaptists, the tradition is that community matters.

340
00:17:36,260 --> 00:17:38,700
What the community thinks about things matters.

341
00:17:38,700 --> 00:17:42,520
This topic is really good for you to talk to each other about as a community.

342
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:44,220
Because what you believe matters.

343
00:17:44,220 --> 00:17:46,340
I can't discern Scripture alone.

344
00:17:46,340 --> 00:17:51,660
I need the help of my brothers and sisters to truly understand the Scriptures.

345
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:59,620
And then, this personal faith of the pietistic world that I am transformed from the inside out.

346
00:17:59,620 --> 00:18:01,700
So, we're deeply relational people.

347
00:18:01,700 --> 00:18:14,500
So, taking us to our heritage, we all kind of whole hog moved from Germany over to America into the Pennsylvania colony in the 1700s.

348
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:20,940
There was a man there by the name of Christopher Sauer who was a printer, a German printer.

349
00:18:20,940 --> 00:18:22,960
And his rival was Ben Franklin.

350
00:18:23,420 --> 00:18:25,580
Ben Franklin was printing in English.

351
00:18:25,580 --> 00:18:27,560
And Christopher Sauer was printing in German.

352
00:18:27,560 --> 00:18:28,720
But they did the same thing.

353
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:31,260
Almanacs, all the kinds of stuff.

354
00:18:31,260 --> 00:18:33,260
But they were printing in different languages.

355
00:18:33,260 --> 00:18:39,400
And Christopher Sauer, over his printing press, in his office, above the heart that's going into his thing,

356
00:18:39,400 --> 00:18:40,900
it had a sign that said,

357
00:18:40,900 --> 00:18:44,340
For the glory of God and for our neighbor's good.

358
00:18:44,340 --> 00:18:48,240
So, this glory of God.

359
00:18:48,240 --> 00:18:51,140
I am part of that tradition that says,

360
00:18:51,140 --> 00:18:52,760
God matters to me personally.

361
00:18:53,240 --> 00:18:55,360
But it also matters what happens in my community.

362
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:59,840
Personal, communal, coming together.

363
00:19:01,420 --> 00:19:02,920
So, let me ground you here.

364
00:19:02,920 --> 00:19:04,200
Philippians chapter 2.

365
00:19:04,200 --> 00:19:08,400
If you would, turn over to your, in your Bibles if you would like.

366
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:10,040
We're going to be all over the place today.

367
00:19:10,040 --> 00:19:10,540
I'll try.

368
00:19:10,540 --> 00:19:14,700
I was told that I could go 50 minutes to an hour by your pastor.

369
00:19:16,760 --> 00:19:20,440
He says, he says, he says he has a really hard time keeping it under an hour and a half.

370
00:19:20,440 --> 00:19:22,540
And yet you like it.

371
00:19:22,540 --> 00:19:27,080
He says a lot of things.

372
00:19:27,080 --> 00:19:27,720
He sure does.

373
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:43,880
So, this is Paul speaking to the Philippians, the church and Philippi, talking about who Jesus is and kind of rooted them in the understanding of how they are supposed to be living out their faith.

374
00:19:45,320 --> 00:19:47,380
Verse 1, chapter 2 of Philippians.

375
00:19:47,380 --> 00:19:58,640
Therefore, if you have any encouragement from being united with Christ, if any comfort from his love, if any common sharing in the spirit, any tenderness and compassion, then make my joy complete.

376
00:19:59,160 --> 00:20:08,300
By being like-minded, having the same love, being one in spirit, one in mind, do nothing out of selfish ambition or vain conceit.

377
00:20:08,300 --> 00:20:15,800
Rather, in humility, value others above yourselves, not looking to your own interests, but each of you to the interests of others.

378
00:20:15,800 --> 00:20:19,120
This is how believers are to walk through this world.

379
00:20:19,120 --> 00:20:21,500
That's challenging.

380
00:20:21,500 --> 00:20:27,940
Verse 5, in your relationships with one another, have the same mindset of Christ.

381
00:20:27,940 --> 00:20:36,380
Now, we're going to ground you where Christ is, and he's telling us we should have the same mind, the same heart, the same spirit as Christ Jesus.

382
00:20:36,380 --> 00:20:49,100
So, this power player, this person coming into a very political world with the Romans, people are begging him to take power, take control.

383
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:52,700
And this is what he does.

384
00:20:53,580 --> 00:21:02,740
He, Christ, who being in the very nature God, did not consider equality with God something to be used to his own advantage.

385
00:21:02,740 --> 00:21:05,500
Rather, he made himself nothing.

386
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:17,540
By taking on the very nature of a servant, being made in human likeness, and being found in appearance as a man, he humbled himself by becoming obedient to death, even death on a cross.

387
00:21:17,540 --> 00:21:24,900
Therefore, God exalted him to the highest place, and gave him the name that is above every name.

388
00:21:24,900 --> 00:21:37,360
That at the name of Jesus, every knee shall bow, in heaven and on earth and under the earth, and every tongue acknowledge that Jesus Christ is Lord to the glory of the God the Father.

389
00:21:39,880 --> 00:21:41,080
This is God's kingdom.

390
00:21:41,080 --> 00:21:42,240
This is the Jesus way.

391
00:21:42,240 --> 00:21:51,980
This is the way, and when we say, before the Civil War, we would say we're Virginian or Texan first, we now say we are Christ first.

392
00:21:51,980 --> 00:21:54,020
We're Christ kingdom first.

393
00:21:54,020 --> 00:21:56,080
The kingdoms don't look like this.

394
00:21:56,080 --> 00:22:05,000
It isn't God's kingdom, and then the American kingdom, or the Roman kingdom, or the, pick a kingdom, I don't care.

395
00:22:05,800 --> 00:22:07,360
It isn't like this.

396
00:22:07,360 --> 00:22:08,640
It is like this.

397
00:22:08,640 --> 00:22:11,180
God's kingdom first.

398
00:22:11,180 --> 00:22:13,160
We are called his children.

399
00:22:13,160 --> 00:22:15,460
We are his citizens first.

400
00:22:15,460 --> 00:22:19,000
And this is the mindset that we take.

401
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:24,360
We don't say we're American first and Jesus second.

402
00:22:24,360 --> 00:22:27,520
We say it the other way around.

